WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:11.690
The Waimakariri District Council meeting for
June started with the acknowledgement of two

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:15.400
Queens Birthday honours, awarded to residents
of our District.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:20.060
Mary Mackintosh received the Queens Service
Medal for her services to the community, including

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:25.660
over 30 years of volunteer work for the Anglican
Parish of Kaiapoi, local orienteering groups

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:28.630
and various school boards in the Kaiapoi area.

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:32.540
Gabrielle Huria was appointed a Member of
the New Zealand Order of Merit for her work

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:37.750
in Pacific and Maori mental health, communications
work with Ngai Tahu and volunteer work in

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:40.630
running film and communications courses for
young Maori.

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:45.350
Post-earthquake, she has been instrumental
in the development of infrastructure and land

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:52.050
zone reform at Tuahiwi, and is currently acting
chair of the Marae.

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:55.870
Council then considered a small parcel of
land in the Rangiora Town Centre which was

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:58.739
acquired for the Red Lion corner road realignment.

00:00:58.739 --> 00:01:02.969
With roading work complete, this land is no
longer needed, however due to its size and

00:01:02.969 --> 00:01:06.930
location, it is unsuitable for commercial
development.

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:11.130
Council approved the land being put into reserve,
which suits the current landscaping and aligns

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:15.520
with the wishes of the Rangiora-Ashley Community
Board.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:19.860
Councillors also approved for the Business
Zones 1 &amp; 2 Public Spaces Policy to be made

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:22.360
available for public consultation.

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:26.610
This policy focuses on making Council’s
public spaces safe and accessible for people

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:27.840
to enjoy.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.720
It establishes regulations for things such
as outdoor dining areas, smoking outdoors,

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:37.770
and links Bylaws and District Plan regulation
for store signage, busking, collecting for

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:39.820
charity and lighting.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:44.440
The policy is expected to be open for public
feedback later this month.

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:51.100
The council also made progress on planning
for an extended kerbside collection service,

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:56.190
which is expected to be introduced once the
Long Term Plan is adopted on June 19.

00:01:56.190 --> 00:02:00.520
Council has the option of owning the new bins,
or the successful kerbside collection contractor

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:02.040
owning the bins.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:06.520
Due to little difference in overall cost and
the administrative and maintenance work required

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:10.520
in owning the bins ourselves, the Council
opted for the latter option.

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:14.640
This will be similar to the current arrangement
in place for our yellow recycling bins.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:19.560
The Council would consider alternative tender bids which include the Council owning the bins.

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:24.960
The next Council Meeting will be on Tuesday
19 June, where the Long Term Plan will be

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:26.960
formally adopted.

